<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas HaMinchah [Laws of the Afternoon Prayer]<br>Halachah 5 (§§ 1–9)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM II:22</span> (<i>Anavah</i> — Humility)</span></div>

<div class="sn">Introduction — True Humility vs. <i>Mochin D'Katnus</i></div>

<div class="p">The matter of <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>:</div>

<div class="p">Based on what Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> writes (<span class="lr">LM II:22</span>) regarding humility [<span class="h">anavah</span>]: in the matter of submission and humility, the world errs greatly — for how much do we exert ourselves in <span class="h">tefilah</span> to leave <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> [contracted consciousness] and enter <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span> [expanded consciousness]! If so, certainly this [mere self-abasement] is not the trait of humility as the world supposes.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — <i>Minchah</i>: Sweetening the <i>Mochin D'Katnus</i> at Their Root</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>. The essential work of man is to leave <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> and enter <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span>. Man's essential service is to break and subdue the evil inclination and its cravings — all the power of the evil inclination and cravings derive from <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>, from which all judgments take hold and the evil inclination's essential power derives (as explained in Rabbainu's words many times). Therefore: <span class="mx">"a person transgresses only if a spirit of folly enters him"</span> — all sins and flaws come through the withdrawal of <span class="h">da'as</span> that derives from <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>. As explained in the teaching "<span class="h">Tik'u Tochachah</span>" (<span class="lr">LM II:8</span>): the desire for adultery (which encompasses all desires) derives from <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Man's essential work is to leave <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> and enter <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span>. Just as a person grows physically — born small, without understanding, gradually growing through nursing until he has a child's awareness, then growing further until at five he has understanding to study Scripture (<span class="mx">"at five — Scripture"</span>), continuing to grow in understanding — so too in Hashem's service: one must constantly labor until one merits leaving <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> for <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span> — breaking all cravings and evil traits (= <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>) and engaging in His service, thereby knowing Him with <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span>. This is man's essential work all his life.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> is exceedingly lofty and the Sages warned about it especially. At <span class="h">Shacharis</span>: <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span>. At night: <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> (= the darkness of night from contracted <span class="h">da'as</span>). Light and darkness are <span class="h">da'as</span> and folly — <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span> and <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>. Day and night are the great luminary and the small luminary: <span class="v">"the great luminary to rule the day, the small luminary..."</span> (= <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span>). All judgments derive from <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> (= darkness), where the evil inclination and all cravings take hold.</div>

<div class="p">Immediately after midday, as the evening shadows incline, the root of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> begins. The essential sweetening is specifically there — sweetening the <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> at the very root of their initial hold (for <span class="mx">"judgment is sweetened only at its root"</span>). This is <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> — the Sages warned greatly. Though the essential rule of judgments is at night, the essential work is at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time specifically — when the day is still great but has begun inclining toward night. Then is the essential sweetening: at the initial root of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>'s hold (after midday, when night's hold begins).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Minchah Gedolah</span> and <span class="h">Minchah Ketanah</span> hint that <span class="h">Minchah</span> encompasses both <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span> — it is still day (<span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span>) yet <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> (= night) has begun stirring. The essential sweetening in completeness is when one merits sweetening them at their supreme root — where all <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span> and <span class="h">d'katnus</span> are encompassed together. Below, in the six directions, there is <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span>; but high above, at the supreme root of the <span class="h">mochin</span>, there is no <span class="h">gadlus</span> or <span class="h">katnus</span> at all — there the essential sweetening is complete. As understood from Rabbainu's teaching "<span class="h">Chadi Rabbi Shimon</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:61</span>): there is sweetening through the particular intellect belonging to each judgment, but the essential sweetening is through the <span class="h">seichel hakolail</span> [the all-encompassing intellect] — the supreme root of the <span class="h">mochin</span>, where all <span class="h">mochin</span> are encompassed together (all is one), from which the essential sweetening comes.</div>

<div class="p">This supreme intellect above all <span class="h">mochin</span> — the world cannot comprehend it; only one who merits it. There is the essential <span class="h">anavah</span>: <span class="mx">"in the place of His greatness, there you find His humility."</span> Humility (which to our understanding is <span class="h">katnus</span> — smallness in one's own eyes) and <span class="h">gadlus</span> are one — in the place of His greatness, there specifically you find humility. This cannot be understood except by one who merits it. This is the perfection of true humility that Moshe Rabbainu <span class="h">alav hashalom</span> achieved — able to write of himself all his great praises, and he wrote: <span class="v">"the man Moshe was exceedingly humble"</span> — yet was truly humble, encompassed in the <span class="h">Ain</span> [Nothingness] where greatness and humility are one: <span class="mx">"in the place of His greatness, there you find His humility."</span> Not everyone merits humility — <span class="mx">"humility is greater than all"</span> (as our Sages say). This is why Rabbainu warns that in the matter of humility, the world errs greatly — certainly humility does not mean being in <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. True humility is being in the ultimate <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span> — and specifically then being in the ultimate humility and self-nullification. Then specifically one merits sweetening all the world's judgments rooted in <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> — the essential sweetening coming from there (= true humility, where <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span> are one). Therefore the essential sweetening of <span class="h">Minchah</span> must be drawn from <span class="h">Minchah</span> of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> — when <span class="h">Ra'ava d'Ra'avin</span> is found (= Moshe's passing = the supreme Will, the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, the supreme root of all <span class="h">mochin</span> where <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span> are encompassed — the essential sweetening).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — Eliyahu: The <i>Erev Rav</i> and the Golden Calf at "Six Hours"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"One should always be careful with <i>Tefilas HaMinchah</i>, for Eliyahu was answered only at <i>Minchah</i>."</span> The essential subduing of idolatry and heresy (= the evil inclination called <span class="mx">"a foreign god"</span>) is through the sweetening achieved at <span class="h">Minchah</span> — for those who merit it. The evil inclination and heresies hold specifically from the initial root of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> (after midday = <span class="h">Minchah</span> time), which derives from the beginning of the contraction (as known: the contraction is the withdrawal of <span class="h">da'as</span> — the root of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> — the essential hold of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> and heresies). Whoever enters inquiries deriving from the beginning of the contraction of the <span class="h">chalal hapanui</span> falls into great heresies, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span> — the beginning of the contraction cannot be grasped by intellect, only by <span class="h">emunah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"the people saw that Moshe was delayed [<i>boshesh</i>] in coming"</span> — <span class="h">ba sheish</span> [the sixth hour arrived]: specifically then, at the beginning of <span class="h">Minchah</span> time, the initial root of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> from the beginning of the contraction. Those who enter inquiries from there fall into great heresies. Therefore then specifically the <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> made the Golden Calf — denying Hashem and Moshe — because they refused to strengthen themselves with <span class="h">emunah</span> and exert themselves to sweeten the judgment through <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>. They entered the inquiries and heresies that derive from there. All because Moshe was not among them: <span class="v">"this Moshe, the man — we do not know what became of him."</span> The heresies from there — only Moshe can sweeten them, being humble with the ultimate humility.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Eliyahu — needing to subdue the fierce idolatry of the Ba'al in Achav's days — was answered only at <span class="h">Minchah</span>. Through <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> he drew the sweetening from the supreme root of the <span class="h">mochin</span> where <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span> are one. Through this the idolatry and heresies from the beginning of the contraction were sweetened and nullified. <span class="v">"All the people saw, fell on their faces, and said: 'Hashem, He is <i>Elokim</i>'"</span> — Hashem (= <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span>) is <span class="h">Elokim</span> (= <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>) — all is one. Through this the idolatry overpowering in Achav's days (deriving from the <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> who sought to overpower at the sixth hour) was subdued. The essential sweetening is through the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (Moshe, Eliyahu) who can ascend to the supreme root where <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span> are one: <span class="v">"Hashem, He is <i>Elokim</i>."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — Accepting a Fast at <i>Minchah</i></div>

<div class="p">The essential acceptance of a fast is at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time: <span class="mx">"any fast not accepted at <i>Minchah</i> is not a fast."</span> All fasts are for <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, and the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is merited through the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Moshe) who can draw the sweetening of <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>. All sins derive from the evil inclination whose power comes from <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>. The essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> (= tikun of all sins) is drawn through the sweetening of <span class="h">Minchah</span> — sweetening all <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> at their supreme root where <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span> are one.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — <i>"Until the Day Blows"</i>: The <i>Sitra Achra</i>'s Hold Begins at Noon</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Until the day blows and the shadows flee"</span> — referring to the flaws and sins Israel committed in the wilderness after all the greatness and goodness they merited there (as Rashi explains). <span class="v">"How do you tend, how do you rest at noon [<i>batzaharayim</i>]"</span> — specifically <span class="h">batzaharayim</span>: the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>'s essential hold is from noon, from <span class="v">"until the day blows"</span> — at noon, at the sun's peak, it immediately begins diminishing, inclining toward the west, toward setting. Then specifically the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> takes hold at the initial root of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>, beginning the instant the day starts turning toward evening (at noon exactly). This is the root of night's darkness (= <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>). The essential sweetening is there — at the initial hold. When one does not merit sweetening there, from there comes the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>'s essential hold. Over this the verse cries: <span class="v">"how do you rest at noon?"</span> — specifically. This is <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>, called in Scripture <span class="mx">"the noon prayer"</span>: <span class="v">"evening and morning and noon I speak and I murmur."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — The Totality of a Man's Life: <i>Shacharis</i>, Noon, Evening</div>

<div class="p">This applies to every person regarding the totality of his life's service. <span class="h">Shacharis</span> [morning] hints to the beginning of one's service of Hashem — at first, things generally proceed in order: praying, studying, engaging in His service (= <span class="h">Shacharis</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">chesed</span>). Then the full order of prayer: <span class="h">korbanos</span>, <span class="h">Pesukay D'Zimrah</span>, <span class="h">Krias Shma</span>, <span class="h">tefilah</span>, <span class="h">tzitzis</span>, <span class="h">tefillin</span> — all the abundance of service each person does at first according to his level. But then usually he falls from his service — Hashem begins testing him, the Adversary attacks (as known to all who wish to enter His service). This is "noon" — <span class="v">"until the day blows"</span> — said of all Israel who merited the great revelations at Sinai and afterward, when the Adversary overpowered, they stumbled. So it is with every person according to his level.</div>

<div class="p">Then tremendous strengthening is needed — beyond all measure — to strengthen oneself each time in His service, never falling from anything in the world (as Rabbainu explains many times). The essential thing: to overcome the <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> that overpower every person. The thoughts of lowliness and smallness that come upon a person through his own sins — though he truly did what he did — on the whole, these thoughts are the evil inclination's tactics. This is not humility at all — it is <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> seeking to overpower and make him fall in his estimation, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, as if he has no hope, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, or to prevent him from some prayer with <span class="h">kavanah</span> and joy. Regarding this, Rabbainu warns: a person must not deceive himself. He must know that Hashem is exceedingly great: <span class="mx">"His greatness is unfathomable"</span> — we do not know at all. (As explained in Rabbainu's discourse at the end of the <span class="h">Sipuray Ma'asiyos</span> — a discourse impossible to explain in writing; only one who merited hearing it can understand its hints.) The principle: no person knows His greatness and mercy, which extend to the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>. Therefore, wherever one has fallen — through His essential greatness and mercy, everything can be completely transformed from bad to good, and sins can become merits (as Rabbainu says explicitly).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 6 — After Midlife: The Essential Battle and the Cry for Hashem's Help</div>

<div class="p">A person must strengthen himself greatly at "noon-time" of his life — in the middle of his years, especially after the majority of his years have passed (= after midday in the span of his life). Usually then the Adversary overpowers more and more. Certainly the essential service should begin in the days of youth specifically — <span class="v">"it is good for a man to bear the yoke from his youth"</span>; <span class="mx">"youth is like a rose-wreath."</span> Rabbainu writes (<span class="h">Alef-Bais</span>, <span class="h">Teshuvah</span> §73): one day a person serves Hashem in his youth is worth more than many years in old age. (We heard from Rabbainu many times: the essential thing is to draw youth specifically to Hashem — the elderly are harder to bring back than the young.) But even one who began Hashem's service in his youth — when he reaches midlife and seeks to attain what he must, the Adversary usually overpowers and spreads itself enormously. How much more so one who never began in his youth — when thoughts of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> come upon him as he sees his years have mostly passed and he must consider his end and purpose — the Adversary immediately overpowers terribly, refusing to let him leave his cravings and the nets in which he is caught.</div>

<div class="p">Over this the verse cries: <span class="v">"tell me, O you whom my soul loves — how do you tend, how do you rest at noon?"</span> — at noon specifically, the aspect of <span class="h">Minchah</span> time (after midday = after the majority of one's years). Therefore one needs great salvation and abundant mercy. Over this <span class="v">"let every devout one pray in his youth"</span> — that Hashem help him to a good end, never falling from His service: <span class="v">"even to old age and white hair, G-d, do not forsake me."</span> As David HaMelech requested: <span class="v">"do not cast me away in old age; when my strength fails, do not forsake me."</span> Sometimes a person falls in old age (as Rabbainu writes [<span class="h">Alef-Bais</span>, <span class="h">N'filah</span> §1]). Therefore one must pray greatly in youth never to fall from His service — especially when the days come and midlife arrives and the day begins to turn, the end approaching: one must look deeply at his end — <span class="mx">"with what face shall he enter before the King?"</span> Then even greater strengthening is needed — the aspect of noon, <span class="h">Minchah</span> time. The days of a man's life are an aspect of day; his death and departure are night and darkness: <span class="v">"before the sun darkens."</span> After midlife: the aspect of <span class="h">Minchah</span> time — one must strengthen and exert oneself all the more, sweetening the judgments (= the evil inclination), binding oneself well to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of the generation (= Moshe), who alone can sweeten <span class="h">Minchah</span>'s judgment at its supreme root. Through this all judgments and the evil inclination's power are nullified — one merits complete <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. Therefore accepting a fast (= <span class="h">teshuvah</span>) is at <span class="h">Minchah</span> specifically.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 7 — <i>Rosh HaShanah</i> as <i>Minchah Gedolah</i> and <i>Minchah Ketanah</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>: <span class="h">Minchah</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">Pachad Yitzchak</span> (Yitzchak established <span class="h">Minchah</span>) = <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. The two days of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> are <span class="h">dina kashya</span> [harsh judgment] and <span class="h">dina rafya</span> [soft judgment] — <span class="h">Minchah Gedolah</span> and <span class="h">Minchah Ketanah</span>. At <span class="h">Minchah Gedolah</span>: <span class="h">dina kashya</span> (the initial root of the contraction's judgment, where the essential overpowering occurs — their power is from the beginning). Therefore the sweetening must be drawn from a higher place — hence <span class="h">Minchah Gedolah</span> [great <span class="h">Minchah</span>]. <span class="h">Minchah Ketanah</span> follows: further from the beginning, they have less power to deceive — hence <span class="h">Minchah Ketanah</span> [small <span class="h">Minchah</span>], requiring less powerful <span class="h">mochin</span> for sweetening.</div>

<div class="p">The two days of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>: the first is <span class="h">dina kashya</span> (the beginning of the contraction — requiring exceedingly great <span class="h">mochin</span>), the essential sweetening from the supreme root where all <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span> and <span class="h">d'katnus</span> are encompassed. On <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, sun and moon (= the great and small luminaries) are encompassed together: <span class="mx">"one remembrance ascends for both"</span> (<span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is both <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Rosh Chodesh</span> = sun and moon). The second day: <span class="h">dina rafya</span> (further from the beginning — not requiring such lofty <span class="h">mochin</span>). In truth, the two days are <span class="mx">"one long day"</span> — all one, the essential sweetening on both days from the supreme root where all is one. Both days (= <span class="h">Minchah Gedolah</span> and <span class="h">Minchah Ketanah</span>, <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span>) are encompassed together — all is one.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the first of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span> — the essential beginning of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> derives from the sweetening of <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>). The essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> of the Ten Days culminates on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — a day of fasting, forgiveness, and atonement. The beginning of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> and the fast of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> starts on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> (the tikun completed on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>). This is accepting a fast at <span class="h">Minchah</span>: the tikun of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> (a fast-day and day of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>) begins at <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> (= <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>). So too any person who wishes to do <span class="h">teshuvah</span> and accept a fast — he must accept it at <span class="h">Minchah</span> specifically (= <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>): the fast (= <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>), the essential fast and <span class="h">teshuvah</span> achieved through the sweetening of <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 8 — Why This Prayer Is Called <i>"Minchah"</i>: The Offering of the Poor Man and <i>Mashiach</i>'s Oil</div>

<div class="p">This resolves the Tosafos' question: why is this prayer called <span class="h">Minchah</span> specifically — the <span class="h">Minchah</span> offering was also brought in the morning (as discussed in the <span class="h">Magen Avraham</span>, <span class="h">siman</span> 232). Based on the above: the <span class="h">Minchah</span> is the offering of the poor man — <span class="v">"if he cannot afford... he shall bring a tenth of an <i>aifah</i>"</span>; our Sages say: <span class="mx">"regarding the animal offering it says 'a fire-offering, a pleasant aroma,' and regarding the <i>Minchah</i> it says 'a fire-offering, a pleasant aroma' — to teach that one who brings much and one who brings little [are equal]."</span></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= the poor man's offering) is the aspect of <span class="h">tefilah l'ani</span> [the poor man's prayer] — exceedingly precious to Hashem (as the Zohar explains at length). The <span class="h">korbanos</span> are the aspect of <span class="h">tefilah</span>; the <span class="h">Tamid</span> offerings (= <span class="h">tefilah</span>) require the <span class="h">Minchah</span> accompaniment — the essential ascent of prayer (= offering) is through the poor man's prayer, which ascends first (as the Zohar explains). Therefore this prayer is called <span class="h">Minchah</span> specifically.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= poor man's offering, the poor man's prayer) — though exceedingly precious to Hashem — still needs great sweetening to complete its work. The poor man is in <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> (= the diminishment of the moon, the small luminary — the source of poverty and want); even his meager sustenance comes with great difficulty and pain, being unable to draw abundance from <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> (= the moon's diminishment = judgments, which delay abundance).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"Poverty follows the poor."</span> But when Hashem wishes to have mercy and save him — the essential salvation is through the exceedingly great true <span class="h">tzadik</span> of the generation, who makes himself poor. As David HaMelech always made himself poor: <span class="v">"answer me, for I am poor and destitute."</span> David knew the poor man's prayer is exceedingly precious — he always made himself poor (as the Zohar explains). This <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= David, <span class="h">Mashiach</span>) who makes himself poor can specifically sweeten the poverty and destitution at its root — sweetening all <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> (even the ultimate <span class="h">katnus</span>) at their supreme root, the <span class="h">Ain</span> above all <span class="h">mochin</span>, where all is one. Though this <span class="h">tzadik</span> makes himself truly poor, joining himself with all the world's poor and lowly — he does not fall, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, into <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>. In his ultimate poverty and lowliness, he is in the ultimate <span class="h">gadlus d'gadlus</span> — for him, <span class="h">gadlus</span> and <span class="h">katnus</span> are one (= the humility of Moshe, <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, David, and all the great true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>).</div>

<div class="p">We see in <span class="h">Mizmor</span> 86: David calls himself poor and destitute (<span class="v">"incline Your ear, Hashem, answer me — for I am poor and destitute"</span>), yet immediately calls himself <span class="h">chassid</span>: <span class="v">"guard my soul, for I am a <i>chassid</i>."</span> We cannot fully understand this — but David merited making himself truly poor while remaining in <span class="h">mochin d'gadlus</span>, calling himself <span class="h">chassid</span>. Such a <span class="h">tzadik</span> alone can help the poor — though he makes himself poor, he never falls into <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>. This is <span class="h">Mashiach</span>: <span class="v">"poor, riding on a donkey"</span> — the salvation of the poor and destitute is through him: <span class="v">"he shall pity the poor and destitute, and save the souls of the needy."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential poverty is poverty of <span class="h">da'as</span>, Torah, and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — distance from Hashem: <span class="mx">"there is no poor man except in <i>da'as</i>."</span> When this poor man sees his lowliness — especially feeling his debts to Hashem through his many sins — and begins pouring out his bitterness before Hashem with a broken, crushed heart, expressing his pain and conversation before Him — certainly his prayer is exceedingly precious and opens all the gates (as explained elsewhere, <span class="h">Hilchos Tefillin</span> 5). But it still lacks the power to complete its work due to the intense judgments (<span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>). The essential salvation is through the great true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= <span class="h">Mashiach</span>) who joins himself with these poor ones — despite their great flaws, his mercy overwhelms; he descends to them, making himself like them and worse — <span class="v">"humbler than any person on the face of the earth."</span> Yet he never falls into <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> — this is the ultimate perfection of Moshe/<span class="h">Mashiach</span>'s humility (which we cannot understand; therefore most of the world errs regarding humility).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"A tenth of an <i>aifah</i> of fine flour for a <i>Minchah</i>, mixed with oil"</span> — the <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= poor man's offering = <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>) must be mixed with oil, to sweeten the judgment. Oil is the light of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>: <span class="h">shemen mishchas kodesh</span> [holy anointing oil] — <span class="h">Mashiach</span> is called so because he was anointed with oil: <span class="v">"therefore G-d, your G-d, has anointed you with oil of joy."</span> The essential sweetening of the <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= poor man's offering = <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>) is through <span class="h">Mashiach</span> (= the oil mixed into the <span class="h">Minchah</span>). Therefore this prayer is called <span class="h">Minchah</span> — its entire tikun being in the aspect of the <span class="h">korban Minchah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 9 — Fasting Sweetens the Days of <i>Katnus</i>; Extra Caution Before <i>Minchah</i></div>

<div class="p">The sweetening through fasting is also in the aspect of <span class="h">Minchah</span>'s sweetening. As Rabbainu writes (<span class="lr">LM I:179</span>): fasting has great tikun — nullifying all types of dispute in general and particular, physical and spiritual. Through fasting, all the dead days are revived — the days in which one sinned and flawed, which lie like dead corpses. When fasting for a day, one has no strength from that day for Hashem's service (due to the fast's weakness) — so one draws strength from the previous day. The more one fasts, the further back one must draw strength — until one who fasts extensively draws from the nursing days (the milk nursed from the mother), elevating even those days.</div>

<div class="p">Through fasting, all the days of <span class="h">katnus</span> and nursing are elevated — the earlier days that were in <span class="h">katnus</span> (as man grows, his mind grows; earlier days are <span class="h">katnus</span> relative to later ones — especially days of sin and neglect). Through fasting, all those days are elevated and repaired, even the days of complete <span class="h">katnus</span> (nursing days). Fasting elevates, repairs, and sweetens all the <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> (= the earlier days). This is the essential aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> — therefore the fast's acceptance is at <span class="h">Minchah</span> specifically.</div>

<div class="p">The Sages warned especially not to begin any work or business before <span class="h">Minchah</span>: <span class="mx">"one should not sit before the barber close to <i>Minchah</i>."</span> The authorities explain that specifically at <span class="h">Minchah</span> one must be more careful — lest one err and forget to pray. Specifically at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time, there is concern and danger of erring more than at any other prayer — because then the judgment from the beginning of the contraction stirs (from where all who retreat from Hashem draw their error). The <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> erred at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time specifically: <span class="v">"the people saw that Moshe was delayed — <i>ba sheish</i>"</span> — from this error and heresy, the evil inclination's entire power derives (distracting man with worldly affairs until he forgets Hashem and His service; the evil inclination's essential power is from heresy and flawed <span class="h">emunah</span>). Therefore at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time one must be all the more careful — not erring, not forgetting to pray and sweeten the harsh judgment. The Sages warned: do not begin work or business before <span class="h">Minchah</span>, for then error is most likely. One must be exceedingly careful to pray <span class="h">Minchah</span> with great <span class="h">kavanah</span>, sweetening the harsh judgment — all through the power of connecting to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (= Moshe, <span class="h">Mashiach</span>), through whom the essential sweetening of <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> comes.</div>

